# Target B: exclude the (2,1,1) case-3 branch by curved descent

Date: 2026-10-03
Base main: 0524c5b4dccc9747d3ebb978f6e82cc9021b912f
Status: proved candidate awaiting promotion; noncanonical. Target B and Level I remain open.

This closes case 3 of `target_b_fourbar_211_phase_group_reduction_2026-10-03.md`
under the same pure-disk candidate hypotheses as the tangent-cubic stack. No
computation or CI output is a proof premise.

## 1. Input

Use the notation of
`target_b_fourbar_211_singleton_stress_endpoint_2026-10-03.md`. Bars h1,h2
share rotation class A; h3,h4 are singleton classes. In case 3,

    n1,n3 != 0,   n2=n4=0.

The persistent-gradient stress polytope is exactly

    P = conv{mu0,mu*},
    mu0=(0,1,0,0),
    mu*=(1,0,k3,k4)/(1+k3+k4),

with k3,k4>0. Hence

    g2=0,
    g1+k3 g3+k4 g4=0.                                   (1)

The weighted cubic at mu* is a nonzero multiple of delta^3. Also h2 has
integer diameter: every separating contribution is +e2 or -e2, and if their
counts are P2,N2,

    D=P2-N2 in Z.                                        (2)

For the sufficiently large Target-B regime D>=4, so P2>=4.

## 2. Negative quadratic correction for h2

Let S+ be the P2 positive separating trains of h2. Restrict g1,g3,g4 to
R^(S+). By (1) their span has dimension at most two. Add the condition

    sum_(tau in S+) a_tau = 0.

Since |S+|>=4, there is a nonzero a supported on S+ with

    g1.a=g3.a=g4.a=0,
    sum a_tau=0.                                         (3)

For h2 every coordinate in S+ has signed projection lambda_(2,tau)=D.
The signed-variance Hessian formula has weighted mean

    abar=D^(-2) sum D a_tau=0,

so

    H2[a,a] < 0.                                         (4)

Thus a is a negative quadratic direction for h2 and is first-order invisible
to h1,h3,h4.

## 3. Cubic correction for the other persistent bars

Let

    C(v)=sum_h mu*_h F_h(v)=c* delta(v)^3,   c* != 0.

Choose v in K with C(v)<0. Perturb inside this open half-space, if needed, to
avoid the proper quadratic kernels of active bars outside the persistent
equality set.

On the face mu2=0, the only normalized nonnegative gradient balance is mu*;
otherwise the exact segment P would contain another point on that face. The
strict linear alternative used in Theorem CG therefore applies to h1,h3,h4.
There is b such that along

    theta(t)=theta(0)+t v+t^3 b

all three bars have strictly negative cubic coefficient. Fix a common negative
margin -eta.

## 4. Common curved path

Use

    theta_eps(t)=theta(0)+t v+eps t^2 a+t^3 b.           (5)

For h1,h3,h4, (3) removes the order-t^2 term from a. Its only change to the
cubic coefficient is eps H_h[v,a]. Choose fixed eps>0 small enough that all
three remain below -eta/2.

Every active bar outside the persistent equality set already has a strictly
negative quadratic coefficient along v. Shrink eps again so those finitely
many inequalities remain strict.

For h2, the zero-gradient persistent-equality calculation in the tangent-cubic
report shows that along every v in K all separating contributions rotate at
one common speed. Factor out that rigid rotation. In the rotating frame the
residual perturbation is eps t^2 a+t^3 b. Since g2=0, (4) gives

    |r2(theta_eps(t))|^2-D^2
      = (eps^2/2) H2[a,a] t^4 + O(t^5) < 0              (6)

for sufficiently small positive t. The a-b cross term starts at t^5 and the
b-quadratic term at t^6.

Thus every active diameter bar decreases on one finite path. Train-angle
deformation preserves the unit rhombus edges. Initially inactive upper
constraints and noncontact lower constraints have positive finite slack, so
they remain feasible for sufficiently small t>0. This contradicts local
diameter minimality.

## 5. Consequence

Case 3 of the fully supported four-persistent-bar (2,1,1) reduction is
impossible for sufficiently large pure strict-rhombic-disk sources in this
candidate stack.

The reduction now has only:

1. all four phase-group rows vanish, so all four persistent bars are exactly
   flat on the neutral train-angle slice; or
2. exactly the two repeated-class bars are nonflat, the singleton bars are
   flat, the persistent stress is unique, and the nonflat response factors
   through one rational relative-rotation coordinate.

The remaining nonflat bottleneck on this subroute is case 2, the one-coordinate
rational-stress core. This note does not prove the Target-B shell inequality
or Level I.
